<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as calibration from './statements/calibration.md';
</script>

An estimator reports an estimate and how sure it is. The estimate can be compared with the truth
when the truth is known. Whether it is as sure as it should be is harder to check, since a single
estimate with a single truth says almost nothing about a covariance. A
[model built forward](../generative-models/) makes the check possible, because it can be run
forward: draw everything, hide part of the draw, estimate the hidden part from the rest, and ask
how often the estimator's own statement of its uncertainty turns out true. This note says why that
works, and how to build the check so that it tests what it is meant to.

## The truth is a draw from the posterior

Draw the quantities $\randomPoint$ and the measurements $\bfz$ together from the model's joint law,
and give an estimator the measurements alone. The estimator's target is the posterior, the
conditional law of $\randomPoint$ given $\bfz$. The hidden $\randomPoint$ is, by the definition of
that conditional law, a draw from it. So a *credible set*, a set that holds a stated posterior
probability $\alpha$, contains the truth with probability $\alpha$, over data sets drawn from the
model.

<Statement {...calibration} />

<Proof>

Conditioning on $\bfz$, $\Pr(\randomPoint \in C(\bfz) \mid \bfz) = \kernel(C(\bfz) \mid \bfz) =
\alpha$ almost surely, by the definition of the kernel as a regular conditional law and the
measurability of the set, and taking the expectation gives $\Pr(\randomPoint \in C(\bfz)) =
\alpha$. Independent pairs give independent indicators, each of mean $\alpha$ by the first part.

</Proof>

Nothing in the statement is about any method. It holds of the exact posterior, and of nothing
else in general: an approximate posterior whose credible sets contain the truth too seldom is
over-confident, and too often, under-confident. The frequency is a property that a posterior has
and an approximation is measured by.

## The credible sets of a Gaussian approximation

A [Laplace approximation](../laplace-approximation/) at a mode $\hat \point$ gives each quantity
$q$, of dimension $d_q$, a marginal that is a normal in the chart at $\hat q$, with a whitening
$W_q$ that elimination computes without forming a covariance. Its credible set of level $\alpha$
is an ellipsoid in that chart, and the truth $q^\star$ lies in it when its *normalized error*

$$
	\varepsilon_q = \bigl\lVert W_q \bigl( q^\star \ominus \hat q \bigr) \bigr\rVert^2 @tag(normalized-error)
$$

is at most $\chi^2_{d_q}(\alpha)$, the $\alpha$-quantile of the chi-squared law with as many
degrees of freedom as $q$ has dimensions. Under the approximation, $\varepsilon_q$ has exactly
that law, so the ellipsoid holds probability $\alpha$.

The ellipsoid holds the exact posterior's probability $\alpha + e(z)$ and not $\alpha$, with
$e(z)$ the approximation's error on that set. Over data sets it therefore contains the truth with
probability $\alpha + \E[e(\bfz)]$, by [%full](statement:calibration) applied to the exact
posterior. A check estimates that probability and compares it with $\alpha$.

## The error to expect

Where the approximation is not exact, $\E[e(\bfz)]$ is not zero, and a check against $\alpha$
alone would pass or fail according to how many data sets it is given: with enough of them, any
error at all is resolved. So the check needs to know how large an error is no fault. Its order is
known. On a curved quantity the approximation's precision
[omits curvature](../laplace-approximation/#what-the-covariance-leaves-out) of a relative size of
the order of the noise's variance in the curved coordinates. Write $\bar\kappa = \sigma_\phi^2$ for
that order, with $\sigma_\phi$ the largest standard deviation of a curved coordinate among the
posterior's marginals. If the exact posterior's covariance is the approximation's times $1 +
\kappa$, then $\varepsilon_q$ is $1 + \kappa$ times a chi-squared variable, and the ellipsoid holds

$$
	\Pr\bigl( \chi^2_{d} \le \chi^2_{d}(\alpha) / (1 + \kappa) \bigr)
	= \alpha - \kappa\, \chi^2_{d}(\alpha)\, f_{d}\bigl(\chi^2_{d}(\alpha)\bigr) + O(\kappa^2) , @tag(coverage-error)
$$

with $f_d$ the chi-squared density. At $\alpha = 0.9$ the slope $\chi^2_d(\alpha)
f_d(\chi^2_d(\alpha))$ is $0.37$ for $d = 6$ and $0.27$ for $d = 3$, so the expected error is
within $\delta_6 = 0.37\, \bar\kappa$ for a quantity of six dimensions and $\delta_3 = 0.27\,
\bar\kappa$ for one of three. An error that is not a multiple of the covariance lies between the
extremes of its eigenvalues, and is bounded the same way.

The same formula says what a mistake looks like. A covariance off by a tenth moves the coverage of
a six-dimensional ellipsoid by about $0.04$, which is far more than a correct approximation's
error at small $\bar\kappa$.

## The check

Take $\alpha = 0.9$, and $N$ independent data sets, each with $n$ quantities of dimension $d$ to
estimate. In data set $r$, let $c_r$ be the fraction of its quantities whose normalized error is
at most $\chi^2_d(\alpha)$. By [%full](statement:calibration) and linearity of expectation,
$\E[c_r] = \alpha + \bar e$, with $\bar e$ the mean error over the quantities, whether or not the
quantities' errors are dependent within a data set, which they are. The data sets are independent,
so the mean $\bar c$ of the $c_r$ has a standard error estimated by $s_* / \sqrt N$, with $s_* =
\max(s, 1 / n)$ and $s$ the sample standard deviation of the $c_r$. The floor $1/n$ is the
resolution of one $c_r$, and keeps the tolerance from collapsing when every data set happens to
give the same fraction. The check passes when

$$
	\lvert \bar c - \alpha \rvert \le 4\, s_* / \sqrt N + \delta_d . @tag(check)
$$

The two parts of the tolerance answer different things. The first is sampling: with $N \ge 100$,
enough for the normal approximation to the mean of independent data sets, an approximation that
erred by exactly $\delta_d$ would fail with probability about $6 \times 10^{-5}$. The second is
the approximation's own expected error, which is known only to its order. So that the verdict does
not hinge on that order's constant, the scales of the scenario are chosen to make the second part
small against the first: $\sigma_\phi \le 0.05$ rad gives $\bar\kappa \le 2.5 \times 10^{-3}$ and
$\delta_6 \le 10^{-3}$, a tenth or less of the sampling part when the fractions spread by a few
hundredths, as fractions over a hundred correlated quantities do.

The check then tests the method and not the approximation. It cannot resolve a calibration error
below its tolerance, and says nothing about one. It does resolve the errors a mistake would make.
The tolerance is fixed by these figures before the data are drawn, and never tuned to the result.
